  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThrough Tuesday: Ridging surface and aloft will remain dominant over \nthe region through Tuesday. Together these features will maintain \nwarmer than normal temperatures and dry weather. The surface high \nwill become centered east of our CWA resulting in a slight increase \nin low level moisture but wl maintain a relatively dry airmass with \na PWAT less than one inch. The main effect will be to help keep \nafternoon minimum relative humidity from bottoming out in the west.\nTemperatures will top out in the lower 90s again Tuesday and the hot \ntemperatures will result in near critically low relative humidity in \nthe east. A \"Limited\" threat for Fire Danger will exist Tuesday \nafternoon in the east, especially considering the ongoing drought \nconditions across the CWA. /22/\n\nWednesday and Thursday...\n\nWeather conditions will be generally quiet and dry across our entire \nforecast area on Wednesday. Near term guidance continues to show a \nmean ridge around 592-594DM that will flatten and build to the \nnortheast. Winds will be light and southerly across our CWA with \nwind gusts under 10 kts. In addition to the light southerly winds, \nsky cover will be generally clear across our CWA on Wednesday. The \ncombination of clear skies and sufficient daytime heating will allow \nfor afternoon highs to peak into the low to mid 90s across the area. \nSeveral spots could see some new record temperatures being set \nespecially if warmer guidance over performs. Wednesday night will \nremain quiet with overnight lows dipping into the low to mid 60s \nunder clear skies. Similar conditions are expected again on Thursday \nwith highs in the low to mid 90s.   \n\nRegarding the fire danger for the rest of this work week, there is \nenough afternoon mixing and drop in afternoon humidity into critical \nlevels (25 to 35 percent range) for a limited fire danger across the \nentire forecast area on Wednesday. An areawide limited risk for fire \ndanger will likely be needed for both Wednesday and Thursday. Deep \nmixing will allow for minimum afternoon relative humidity levels to \ndip (20 to 30 percent range) most afternoons throughout the week, so \nlimited fire danger should persist, especially given light rain \ntotals from Isaias. High pressure will keep winds relatively light \nand should limit greater fire concerns throughout the work week.\n\nFriday through Monday...\n\nA stout dry cold front will push into the region by Friday and will \ncause dewpoints to fall into the mid 30s to mid 40s. At the very \nleast, cool down still remains likely next weekend. Highs will be \nseasonably warm Friday in the upper 80s to low 90s, with lows in the \nlow to mid 60s that morning and lows that night upper 40s to low 50s \nnorth of Interstate 20 (48-52F) to mid to upper 50s (53-57F) to the \nsouth. Seasonable and pleasant fall temperatures will build areawide \ninto next weekend. \n\nThe combination of continued drought stressed vegetation and \nlongstanding limited rainfall will help increase fire danger \nconcerns. 1035-1045mb surface high building will build east out of \nthe lee side of the Rockies through the Mid West, leading to 6-8mb \npressure gradient and resultant gusty gradient wind/wind gusts \nreaching 30 mph at times. This will be a concern and a fire danger \ngraphic/Elevated risk will likely be needed if these trends persist. \nFire danger concerns will continued to be closely monitored as we \nget closer to the weekend. Low chances for scattered showers (PoPs \naround 15%) will be possible across portions of west MS, southwest \nMS, and portions of northeast LA heading into Monday. /CR/",
